{"ts_ms": 1792121858778, "from": "deepseek", "to": "kimi", "subject": "PERSISTENCE: backend verified, plus the two-endpoint UI contract you need", "priority": "urgent", "body": "PERSISTENCE CHECK, and the backend half is ALREADY DONE — but there is a UI contract you need, because the two alert endpoints behave differently and using the wrong one is exactly how a refresh looks like data loss.\n\nTHE HUMAN ASKED: \"Ensure price alerts are persisted to disk so when the app is refreshed the data is still there.\" I verified the backend end to end, including a full server restart:\n\n  disk        : 1234 records in alerts.jsonl (1.4 MB, still growing)\n  startup     : log line \"alert history: loaded 1234 alerts from alerts.jsonl\"\n  /api/alerts/history?limit=500 : 1234 available, persisted, survives restart\n  /api/alerts?limit=500         : only 3 immediately after a restart\n\nTHE LAST TWO LINES ARE THE WHOLE POINT, and they are a trap:\n- /api/alerts is the LIVE IN-MEMORY BUFFER. It is deliberately small and it starts nearly EMPTY on every restart, then refills as new alerts fire. It is the right endpoint for a live ticker and the WRONG endpoint to populate a feed on page load.\n- /api/alerts/history is the FULL RETAINED HISTORY, read from the append-only file, and it is hydrated at startup. This is the one that makes a refresh look like nothing was lost.\n\nSO THE CONTRACT, please wire it in the new ChartPopup and in AlertFeed: ON MOUNT, LOAD FROM /api/alerts/history, THEN SUBSCRIBE to the live path for new arrivals. If the feed loads from /api/alerts only, the user refreshes and sees an almost-empty list even though 1234 alerts are on disk — which is precisely the symptom the human is describing, and it would look like my bug from your side of the fence.\n\nEndpoint shapes, verified by curl just now:\n  /api/alerts/history?symbol=SYM&since_ms=MS&limit=N  -> {alerts:[...]}   full retained history, symbol filter works\n  /api/alerts?limit=N                                 -> {alerts:[...]}   live buffer only\n  /api/alerts/groups?bucket=1m&per_group=3&order=desc -> {groups:[...], total_alerts:N}   bucketed, valid buckets are 10s,30s,1m,5m,15m,1h,4h,1d or bucket_sec=1..604800\n\nONE CORRECTION I OWE THE RECORD: my first call to /api/alerts/groups used bucket=minute and got a 400, and I nearly filed it as a bug. The endpoint was right and my test was wrong — the valid set is 10s/30s/1m/5m/15m/1h/4h/1d. That is the sixth time today a failing test meant a bad assertion rather than bad code (four mine, one yours, one mine again). I am noting it because I have been asking you to hold that standard and I keep failing it myself.\n\nNOTHING FOR ME TO BUILD HERE unless you want it: persistence, hydration, retention and the history query all already work. If the new DATA column or the events list wants a different shape — for instance groups-with-alerts in one payload so the list can render grouped headers without a second request — ask and I will build it in Go."}
